{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Deriv","lean:module:Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.InfiniteSum.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.NumberTheory.ZetaValues","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":23,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo_duplication","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo_duplication","kind":"theorem","line":357,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L357-L402","doc":"**Duplication (Landen) identity** `Li₂(x) + Li₂(-x) = ½ Li₂(x²)` for `|x| < 1`.\nGeneralises `LiTwo_neg_one` (the `x = 1` boundary case).  The combined summand\nvanishes on even indices; its odd reindex `k = 2j+1` collapses `x^{2j+2}` to\n`(x²)^{j+1}` and halves the weight, via the even/odd\n`Function.Injective.hasSum_iff` reindex + `HasSum.unique`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.liTwo_summand_summable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.liTwo_summand_summable","kind":"theorem","line":344,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L344-L355","doc":"Absolute summability of the `LiTwo` summand on the open unit disc: dominated,\nterm by term, by the geometric series `Σ |y|^{k+1}` (each `(k+1)⁻² ≤ 1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_log_eq_mseries","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_log_eq_mseries","kind":"theorem","line":305,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L305-L331","doc":"**Third Part-A reduction (the `m`-series):** interchanging the finite `k`-sum\nwith the `m`-tsum (`Summable.tsum_finsetSum`) and summing the geometric\n`k`-series (`geom_sum_eq`) puts `log Φ_n(x; q)` in closed `m`-series form\n\n`log Φ_n(x; q) = Σ'_m  x^{m+1}/(m+1) · (q^{(m+1)n} − 1)/(q^{m+1} − 1)`.\n\nThis is the exact, finite-`n` object whose `q → 1⁻` limit is `Li₂(x)`: each\n`(q^{(m+1)n} − 1)/(q^{m+1} − 1) → n → ∞`-regularised factor tends to `1/(m+1)`\nas `q → 1⁻`, turning the summand into `x^{m+1}/(m+1)²`.  The termwise limit +\ndominated-convergence exchange with the `m`-sum is the remaining analytic core\n(the `ArchimedeanLimit` class), NOT discharged here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_log_eq_tsum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_log_eq_tsum","kind":"theorem","line":288,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L288-L303","doc":"**Second Part-A reduction:** each per-factor log becomes the `Li`-type power\nseries, so `log Φ_n(x; q) = Σ_{k<n} Σ'_{m} (x qᵏ)^{m+1}/(m+1)`.  Requires each\nFaddeev factor to be both positive and inside the radius of convergence\n(`|x qᵏ| < 1`), automatic for `x ∈ [0,1)`, `q ∈ [0,1]`.  This is the exact,\nfinite-`n` reduction of the log partial product to the double `(k, m)` series\nwhose `q → 1⁻` limit is `Li₂`; the outstanding Part-A input is the uniform\n(Tauberian) exchange of that limit with the `m`-sum, still carried by\n`ArchimedeanLimit`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_log_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_log_eq","kind":"theorem","line":276,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L276-L286","doc":"**First Part-A reduction:** the log of the partial product is the finite sum\nof the per-factor logs, `log Φ_n(x; q) = Σ_{k<n} -log(1 - x qᵏ)`, whenever\nevery factor is positive (guaranteed for `x ∈ [0,1)`, `q ∈ [0,1]`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.denomPolyLocal_eq_prod","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.denomPolyLocal_eq_prod","kind":"theorem","line":268,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L268-L274","doc":"The recursive `denomPolyLocal` is the finite product of Faddeev factors\n`∏_{k<n} (1 - x qᵏ)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.neg_log_one_sub_hasSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.neg_log_one_sub_hasSum","kind":"theorem","line":260,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L260-L266","doc":"**Per-factor log expansion.** Each Faddeev factor contributes the classical\nseries `-log(1 - y) = Σ_{m≥1} yᵐ/m` for `|y| < 1` (Mathlib\n`hasSum_pow_div_log_of_abs_lt_one`).  Applied with `y = x qᵏ` this is the\nper-crossing term of the archimedean limit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":198,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L198-L246","doc":"**Bar-odd cusp partner** `LiTwo (-1) = -π²/12`.  The alternating Basel value\n`-η(2) = -ζ(2)/2`, the natural `sinh`/bar-odd companion of the bar-even\n`LiTwo 1 = π²/6`.  Proved by the duplication `LiTwo 1 + LiTwo (-1) = π²/12`:\nthe combined summand `g n = ((-1)^{n+1} + 1)/(n+1)²` vanishes on even `n`, and\nits odd reindex `n = 2k+1` is `½·(k+1)⁻²`, so `∑ g = ½·ζ(2) = π²/12` (the\neven/odd `Function.Injective.hasSum_iff` reindex, as in Mathlib's\n`hasSum_log_sub_log_of_abs_lt_one`).  Subtracting `∑ (n+1)⁻² = ζ(2)` leaves\n`LiTwo (-1) = π²/12 - π²/6 = -π²/12`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo_one","kind":"theorem","line":184,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L184-L196","doc":"**The cusp value is `ζ(2) = π²/6`.** Closes the last analytic input of the\nPart-B derivation: the residual `LiTwo 1` left after the pole-subtraction and\nthe `T_w` Frobenius truncation is the Basel constant, obtained here from\nMathlib's `hasSum_zeta_two` (Basel problem, `∑' n:ℕ, 1/n² = π²/6`) by splitting\noff the vanishing `n = 0` term.  This is the extended-Bloch / Rogers regulator\nconstant `ζ(2) = Li₂(1)` that Part B identifies as the (quantum-)dilogarithm\ninversion constant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo_one_eq_tsum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo_one_eq_tsum","kind":"theorem","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L173-L182","doc":"**The cusp value `LiTwo 1` is the Basel series** `∑' 1/(k+1)²` (definitional:\n`1^(k+1) = 1`). This isolates the entire remaining analytic input of Part B to\na single named Mathlib result: `LiTwo 1 = π²/6` follows from\n`hasSum_zeta_two` (`Mathlib.NumberTheory.ZetaValues`, the Basel problem —\n`∑' n:ℕ, 1/n² = π²/6`, whose `n=0` term is `0`), the extended-Bloch / Rogers\nregulator constant `ζ(2) = Li₂(1)` that Part B derives as the quantum-dilog\ninversion constant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.log_neg_factor_split","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.log_neg_factor_split","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L156-L171","doc":"**Pole-subtraction (algebraic core of Part B).** For a negative factor\n`1 − w < 0` (i.e. `w > 1`), the principal `log|1 − w| = log(w − 1)` splits as\nthe **algebraic pole** `log w` plus the convergent remainder `log(1 − w⁻¹)`:\n\n`log(w − 1) = log w + log(1 − w⁻¹)`.\n\nThis is exactly the subtraction that regularizes each `k > k*` factor in the\nPart-B continuation (the `+iπ` branch phase lives on the complex side and is\nthe Faddeev modular anomaly; here we isolate the real magnitude). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.archimedeanLimit_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.archimedeanLimit_zero","kind":"instance","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L129-L144","doc":"**Unconditional instance at `x = 0`.** The Faddeev limit holds\ntrivially at the boundary point `x = 0` because both sides of\nthe asserted convergence are identically zero, so the limit is\nthe constant zero sequence. This is the only point at which the\n`ArchimedeanLimit` class can be inhabited without further\nMathlib infrastructure; for every `x ∈ (0, 1)` the instance\nrequires the full Tauberian argument from Faddeev 1994 /\nKashaev 1997. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_to_LiTwo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_to_LiTwo","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L119-L127","doc":"Stated form of the conjecture (sorry-free at the level of Lean,\ninhabited only via a `[ArchimedeanLimit x]` typeclass instance). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.ArchimedeanLimit.faddeev_limit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.ArchimedeanLimit.faddeev_limit","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L113-L113","doc":"The Faddeev limit of the rescaled log partial product equals\nthe classical dilogarithm. The hypotheses on `(q, n)` capture\nthe joint scaling `q → 1`, `n → ∞`, `n(1-q) → ∞`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.ArchimedeanLimit.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.ArchimedeanLimit.mk","kind":"ctor","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L108-L108","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.ArchimedeanLimit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.ArchimedeanLimit","kind":"class","line":104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L104-L117","doc":"The Archimedean-limit conjecture, packaged as a typeclass.\nA concrete instance asserts the convergence\n`(1 - q) · log Φ_n(x; q) → Li₂(x)` along Faddeev's scaling. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:conj:dilogarithm-archimedean-limit","label":"conj:dilogarithm-archimedean-limit","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/dilogarithm-archimedean-limit.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.faddeev_limit_at_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.faddeev_limit_at_zero","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L92-L102","doc":"Boundary case of the Faddeev limit: at `x = 0` the rescaled\nlogarithm of the partial product is `0` for every `(q, n)`,\nmatching `LiTwo 0 = 0`. This is the only point at which the\nFaddeev limit is unconditional in Lean (no Tauberian input\nrequired). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_zero","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L83-L90","doc":"Boundary lemma: at `x = 0` the partial product is identically\n`1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly_zero","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L73-L81","doc":"Boundary lemma: at `x = 0` every factor `(1 - x q^k) = 1`, so\n`denomPolyLocal q 0 n = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo_zero","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L66-L71","doc":"Boundary lemma: `LiTwo 0 = 0`. Each term in the series has\n`0 ^ (k+1) = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.LiTwo","kind":"def","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L56-L64","doc":"The classical real dilogarithm series `Σ_{k≥1} x^k / k²`,\ngiven as a `tsum`. On the closed unit disc the series converges\nabsolutely and equals the principal-branch real dilogarithm\n`Real.dilog` (when Mathlib provides it). The `tsum` is `0`\noutside the radius of convergence by Mathlib's convention,\nwhich suffices for the boundary lemmas below. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.partialProductLocal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.partialProductLocal","kind":"def","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L51-L54","doc":"Local restatement of the partial product. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.denomPolyLocal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.denomPolyLocal","kind":"def","line":42,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit.lean#L42-L49","doc":"Local restatement of the q-Pochhammer denominator, kept self-\ncontained so this Lean file does not depend on its sibling\n`q-pochhammer-partial-product.lean`. The two definitions agree\nby inspection. ","signatureChunk":0}]}